  "summary": "Six November preprints tackle error-prone steps in sequence reconstruction and search, from codon alignment to signal-level assembly and GPU RNA homology search.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is revising a stage of sequence reconstruction or search that usually passes errors downstream. WALRUS in HyPhy 3 estimates codon alignment, phylogeny and selection tests jointly, reducing indel-driven false positives in simulated and avian data. HARICOT assembles raw nanopore signals without basecalling, using fuzzy de Bruijn graphs and Hough-transform overlap detection. Other work compares local assembly consensus strategies for long-read structural variant calling, finding that performance depends on read type and error profile. A further benchmark shows that polymerase choice affects HiFi assembly contiguity and completeness. TERRACE 2.0 combines outward and chimeric reads for circular RNA assembly. Infernal-GPU parallelizes covariance-model scoring and recalibrates E-values. Together these items span assembly, alignment and homology search, with an emphasis on error control and sensitivity.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
}
